diagnosis_text:
A full multi-layer diagnostic sweep found no fault anywhere in the topology. Phase 1 (`get_reachability`) confirmed that `host_1_1_1_1` reaches all data-center IP addresses (web_server_0 through web_server_3 and dns_server) at 0% packet loss. Phase A infrastructure sweeps (`infra_sweep`, `l2_snapshot`) found no ACL drop rules, no bad ARP entries, no duplicate MACs, and all hosts possess correct IPs and default routes. Phase B OSPF/FRR health checks showed all six routers with daemons active, all neighbors in Full state, and nine OSPF routes consistently installed on every router — the control plane and data plane path from subnet 10.1.1.0/24 through core routers to the 10.200.0.0/24 server subnet is intact. Phase D service checks (`service_snapshot`, `pressure_sweep`) showed that `host_1_1_1_1` successfully resolves all four web hostnames (web0.local–web3.local) via the DNS server at 10.200.0.2, and receives HTTP 200 responses from each web server in approximately 4 ms — with no resource pressure on any device. There is no evidence in live traffic, routing state, interface counters, service responses, or log traces of any fault. The user's reported symptom ("data center vanished") cannot be reproduced or traced to any network or service defect at the time of this investigation. Root cause: no anomaly is present; the network is fully operational. Fix: no corrective action is required — verify that the user is using the correct hostnames or application endpoint, and confirm whether the issue was transient and self-resolved before this investigation began.
